# Named Exports with Default Export Object

Yes! You can create a default export object that contains all your functions. Here are a few approaches:

## Option 1: Define functions, then export as default object

```javascript
function doSomething() {
  console.log('doing something');
}

function doSomethingElse() {
  console.log('doing something else');
}

function anotherFunction() {
  console.log('another function');
}

const Util = {
  doSomething,
  doSomethingElse,
  anotherFunction
};

export default Util;
```

## Option 2: Define object with functions directly

```javascript
const Util = {
  doSomething() {
    console.log('doing something');
  },
  doSomethingElse() {
    console.log('doing something else');
  },
  anotherFunction() {
    console.log('another function');
  }
};

export default Util;
```

## Option 3: Export both named AND default (most flexible)

```javascript
export function doSomething() {
  console.log('doing something');
}

export function doSomethingElse() {
  console.log('doing something else');
}

const Util = {
  doSomething,
  doSomethingElse
};

export default Util;
```

This gives you flexibility to import either way:

```javascript
// Use as namespace object
import Util from './util.js';
Util.doSomething();
Util.doSomethingElse();

// OR import individual functions
import { doSomething, doSomethingElse } from './util.js';
doSomething();
```

## Usage Example

```javascript
// util.js
export default {
  doSomething() {
    console.log('Something!');
  },
  doSomethingElse() {
    console.log('Something else!');
  }
};

// main.js
import Util from './util.js';

Util.doSomething();      // "Something!"
Util.doSomethingElse();  // "Something else!"
```

